From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Louis Sautier <sautier.louis@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 21:13:16 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 453/455] scsi: mpt3sas: Fix out-of-bounds cpu_msix_table read
 on CPU hotplug

mpt3sas_base_attach() allocates cpu_msix_table based on the highest CPU
online at probe time, but _base_get_msix_index() indexes it with
raw_smp_processor_id() without a bounds check. Once a CPU with a higher id
comes online, I/O submitted from it causes an out-of-bounds read of the
table. The value read is sent to the firmware as the reply queue
index. When it is not a valid queue, the IOC faults and I/O fails.

On a SAS3224 (FW 16.00.01.00) running v7.4-rc3 with KASAN, booted with
maxcpus=1 and CPUs 1-95 onlined later, read-only fio pinned to each CPU in
turn triggers, on CPU 1:

  BUG: KASAN: slab-out-of-bounds in _base_get_msix_index+0x1de/0x230 [mpt3sas]
  Read of size 1 at addr ffff88812fdf51c1 by task kworker/1:1H/1528

and, on CPU 16:

  mpt3sas_cm0: fault_state(0x1500)!
  mpt3sas_cm0: sending diag reset !!
  I/O error, dev sda, sector 3873068048 op 0x0:(READ) flags 0x800000 phys_seg 1 prio class 2
  mpt3sas_cm0: removing unresponding devices: start
  sd 0:0:0:0: [sda] Synchronizing SCSI cache

Allocate the table based on nr_cpu_ids instead, and map every CPU in each
reply queue's affinity mask, including offline ones. Managed IRQ affinity
already spreads the vectors over all possible CPUs, so a CPU onlined later
uses the queue whose affinity mask contains it.

The online-mask restriction and the bounds check added by commit
4a8842de8db4 ("scsi: mpt3sas: fix an out of bound write") are no longer
needed, since the table now has an entry for every possible CPU.

When smp_affinity_enable=0 or a reply queue has no affinity mask, the
fallback path still sets only the entries of the CPUs online at probe
time. A CPU onlined later then reads 0 from the zeroed table, and queue 0
is always a valid queue.

With the change, the same fio test runs without errors, also while CPUs
1-95 are taken offline and back online in a loop. With maxcpus=12 (12 reply
queues), I/O from each of the 96 CPUs completes on the vector whose
affinity mask contains that CPU.

Fixes: f92363d12359 ("[SCSI] mpt3sas: add new driver supporting 12GB SAS")
Cc: stable@vger.kernel.org
Signed-off-by: Louis Sautier <sautier.louis@gmail.com>
Link: https://patch.msgid.link/20261118201316.326943-1-sautier.louis@gmail.com
Signed-off-by: Martin K. Petersen (Oracle) <mkp@kernel.org>
(cherry picked from commit 14bc5d239dddb3ce1264de74b8460c0a61745563)
Signed-off-by: Thomas Lamprecht <t.lamprecht@proxmox.com>
---
 drivers/scsi/mpt3sas/mpt3sas_base.c | 16 ++++++++--------
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 8 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/scsi/mpt3sas/mpt3sas_base.c b/drivers/scsi/mpt3sas/mpt3sas_base.c
index ae2f7d9c281f920fdaaa189fc6c04b257b9759f9..c4977d4480fb0143baec94b1c28b3c185415526b 100644
--- a/drivers/scsi/mpt3sas/mpt3sas_base.c
+++ b/drivers/scsi/mpt3sas/mpt3sas_base.c
@@ -3264,11 +3264,8 @@ _base_assign_reply_queues(struct MPT3SAS_ADAPTER *ioc)
 				goto fall_back;
 			}
 
-			for_each_cpu_and(cpu, mask, cpu_online_mask) {
-				if (cpu >= ioc->cpu_msix_table_sz)
-					break;
+			for_each_cpu(cpu, mask)
 				ioc->cpu_msix_table[cpu] = reply_q->msix_index;
-			}
 		}
 		return;
 	}
@@ -8414,15 +8411,18 @@ int
 mpt3sas_base_attach(struct MPT3SAS_ADAPTER *ioc)
 {
 	int r, i, rc;
-	int cpu_id, last_cpu_id = 0;
 
 	dinitprintk(ioc, ioc_info(ioc, "%s\n", __func__));
 
 	/* setup cpu_msix_table */
 	ioc->cpu_count = num_online_cpus();
-	for_each_online_cpu(cpu_id)
-		last_cpu_id = cpu_id;
-	ioc->cpu_msix_table_sz = last_cpu_id + 1;
+	/*
+	 * Allocate an entry for every possible CPU, because
+	 * _base_get_msix_index() indexes the table with
+	 * raw_smp_processor_id(), which can be a CPU brought online after
+	 * the adapter was attached.
+	 */
+	ioc->cpu_msix_table_sz = nr_cpu_ids;
 	ioc->cpu_msix_table = kzalloc(ioc->cpu_msix_table_sz, GFP_KERNEL);
 	ioc->reply_queue_count = 1;
 	if (!ioc->cpu_msix_table) {
